From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:43:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add retained meshes (Window::paint_mesh)

A Path is rebuilt and written into the frame's instance buffer every frame
it is painted, so a canvas of many shapes costs its vertices every frame:
about 10 ns per vertex on the CPU and ~100 bytes of instance data per
vertex, which at a million vertices is 20-100 ms a frame.

A Mesh is an immutable triangle list with a colour per vertex (16 bits per
channel). Window::paint_mesh(mesh, transformation, paint) paints it with a
transformation to window pixels and a paint (any Background) its vertex
colours are multiplied by: white draws the vertex colours, translucent
white fades them, a pattern or gradient is drawn in them. The Metal
renderer uploads a mesh the first time it is painted and keeps it on the
GPU while it is in use, so painting it again costs a small record whatever
its size:

* MeshStore keeps mesh vertices in a few shared buffers ("pages", added as
  needed, never copied), in blocks of 48 vertices allocated from a free list.
  A mesh is dropped from the GPU once every Mesh handle is gone (weak
  handles, swept every 32 frames) or, beyond a 256 MB budget, when it was
  painted least recently. Blocks are reused only after the frames that drew
  them completed on the GPU (command buffer completion handlers).
* A batch of meshes is one instanced draw call: an instance per block
  (MeshChunk), 48 vertices each, transformed and clipped in the vertex
  shader; vertices past a draw's last block collapse. The CPU writes one
  record per draw and 12 bytes per block.
* Meshes are rasterized like paths, into the multisampled path texture
  (the same pipeline setup, sample count and blending), then composited
  once over the union of their bounds, so they are anti-aliased exactly
  like paths. Draws keep their paint order within a batch, and sprites
  painted between meshes (a canvas's text) are rasterized into the same
  pass in order, so interleaved meshes and text cost one pass instead of
  one per switch.

The wgpu and DirectX renderers rasterize meshes through their path pipeline
(MeshDraw::path_vertices transforms the vertices on the CPU every frame),
which is correct but costs what paths cost. Retained-buffer entry points
for them are included: WGSL (vs_mesh/fs_mesh in the storage-buffer
transport, validated by the naga tests) and HLSL (mesh_vertex/
mesh_fragment, not compiled here), not wired to pipelines yet.

Window::render_offscreen (test-support) renders the last drawn frame the
way presenting it does, for benchmarks of windows that are not on screen.

